1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,620
One fall on the porch steps.

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:03,600
Six nights in the hospital.

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:05,580
Three weeks of rehab.

4
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:08,730
On one Medicare card, that year costs you

5
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:09,760
two hundred eighty-three dollars.

6
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:13,640
On another… three thousand seven hundred

7
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:16,230
seventy-five. And the plan with the lowest

8
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:17,180
cap on the shelf…

9
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:19,100
costs the most of all.

10
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:21,560
This is Run the Number.

11
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:25,570
One bad year, three cards, every line added

12
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:26,420
up on screen.

13
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:28,570
By the end you'll know what the hospital

14
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:31,090
stay itself can cost, what the rest of

15
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,530
the year adds, and the three lines in your

16
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,240
plan's chart that decide it.

17
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,440
This is education, not advice.

18
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:39,440
Nobody here sells insurance.

19
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,670
Start with the six nights, because that's

20
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:43,520
the biggest line.

21
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:46,570
On Original Medicare, a hospital stay runs

22
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:47,600
through Part A.

23
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:50,280
You pay one deductible for the whole stay.

24
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:52,810
Seventeen hundred thirty-six dollars for

25
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:55,250
twenty twenty-six, and it covers up to

26
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:57,810
sixty days. The twenty twenty-seven figure

27
00:00:57,860 --> 00:00:59,020
comes out this month.

28
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,290
On a Medicare Advantage plan, the hospital

29
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:03,320
is a copay.

30
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,610
Usually so much a day for the first few

31
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:06,940
days, then nothing.

32
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,740
Three hundred a day, four hundred a day…

33
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,080
the chart tells you.

34
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:12,640
How high can that go?

35
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,340
That's not up to the plan.

36
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:15,700
It's up to Medicare.

37
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,810
Every spring, Medicare publishes a table

38
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:20,810
of the most a plan is allowed to charge

39
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:22,140
for each kind of care.

40
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,990
For twenty twenty-seven, a six-day stay

41
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,130
on a plan with the standard cap can run

42
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:29,000
up to two thousand four hundred fifty dollars.

43
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:32,570
Most plans sit under that line, but that's

44
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,450
the ceiling, so that's where we'll run

45
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,970
this year: at the most a plan is allowed

46
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:39,460
to charge. Your chart has your number.

47
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,280
And here's the part almost nobody reads.

48
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:44,950
A plan that advertises a low cap…

49
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,020
is allowed higher copays.

50
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:49,370
Medicare's own table says so.

51
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,950
The same six nights on a plan with a cap

52
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,190
under forty-five hundred can run up to

53
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:55,840
three thousand sixty-two.

54
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,070
So the cap on the front of the brochure

55
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,670
and the copay inside the chart move in

56
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,040
opposite directions. Remember that.

57
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:05,000
How to check yours.

58
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:07,790
Open the Evidence of Coverage, find the

59
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:10,010
line that says inpatient hospital care,

60
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:12,870
and write down two things: the copay per

61
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,940
day, and how many days it applies.

62
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:17,720
Now the rest of the year.

63
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,040
Because the hospital is where it starts,

64
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:21,440
not where it ends.

65
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:23,820
After six nights comes rehab.

66
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:26,580
Twenty days in a skilled nursing facility.

67
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:29,450
On the standard-cap plan, Medicare's table

68
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,920
says days one through twenty can be zero.

69
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,370
On the low-cap plan, up to twenty dollars

70
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:35,920
a day. Four hundred.

71
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:37,730
Then the follow-up.

72
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:39,700
Twelve physical therapy visits.

73
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,230
Fifty-five dollars each on the standard-cap

74
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,470
plan, ninety on the low-cap plan.

75
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:47,120
Six hundred sixty, or one thousand eighty.

76
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:49,180
Six specialist visits.

77
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,170
Sixty each, or ninety-five.

78
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:53,420
Three sixty, or five seventy.

79
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,680
Four primary care visits.

80
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:57,260
Forty, or seventy.

81
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:59,180
One sixty, or two eighty.

82
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:01,990
A walker. Twenty percent of the cost on

83
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:03,880
one plan, fifty on the other.

84
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,320
Call it thirty dollars, or seventy-five.

85
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,530
And the emergency room the night of the

86
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,000
fall. One fifteen, or one fifty.

87
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:12,590
Add it up.

88
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,840
The standard-cap plan: three thousand seven

89
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:15,940
hundred seventy-five dollars.

90
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,410
The low-cap plan: five thousand six hundred

91
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:22,470
seventeen… which hits its cap, so you pay

92
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:24,930
forty-four fifty. The plan with the smaller

93
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:25,880
number on the front…

94
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:27,980
costs seven hundred dollars more.

95
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:31,120
Now Original Medicare, same year.

96
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,020
The Part A deductible, seventeen thirty-six,

97
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:36,250
covers the hospital and the first twenty

98
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:37,100
days of rehab.

99
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:39,610
Then Part B: a two hundred eighty-three

100
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,190
dollar deductible, and twenty percent of

101
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:44,000
what Medicare approves for the doctors,

102
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:45,960
the therapy, the walker.

103
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,460
Say five thousand dollars of approved bills…

104
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:49,780
that's about twelve hundred.

105
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,580
The year comes to about twenty-nine sixty.

106
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,600
And Original Medicare with a Plan G supplement:

107
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,930
the supplement pays all of that except

108
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,200
the Part B deductible.

109
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:00,580
Two hundred eighty-three dollars.

110
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:03,290
Plus the premium every month, which is

111
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:04,920
the trade we ran last episode.

112
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:09,490
Four cards. Four years.

113
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:12,240
Two eighty-three. Twenty-nine sixty.

114
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:15,040
Thirty-seven seventy-five. Forty-four fifty.

115
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:17,660
Every one of those is public.

116
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,750
Three of them are in a chart you already

117
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,860
have. What does that do to the money?

118
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:24,510
A thirty-seven-hundred-dollar year is nearly

119
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,570
four months of a thousand-dollar-a-month

120
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:27,600
draw from savings.

121
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,270
Three years like it in a decade, and a

122
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:32,030
full year of withdrawals is gone…

123
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:34,300
to copays you could have read in advance.

124
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:35,940
How to check yours.

125
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:38,150
Three lines in the benefits chart.

126
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,270
Inpatient hospital: the copay per day and

127
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:42,180
the number of days.

128
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:45,030
Skilled nursing facility: the copay for

129
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:46,280
days one through twenty.

130
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:48,640
Physical therapy and specialist visits:

131
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:50,320
the copay per visit.

132
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:52,870
Write the three numbers on one card, next

133
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:54,950
to the cap, and you have the real price

134
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,580
of a bad year on that plan.

135
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:58,220
Then do one more thing.

136
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:01,190
Take the card to the plan you're thinking

137
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:03,380
of switching to, and fill it in again.

138
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,710
Same fall, same six nights, same three

139
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,270
weeks. The plan that wins that card is

140
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,080
the plan that's cheaper when it matters…

141
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:12,660
whatever the cap says.

142
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,030
A fee-only fiduciary or a CPA can run your

143
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:18,050
own numbers, and the State Health Insurance

144
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,790
Assistance Program will walk you through

145
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:20,880
the chart for free.

146
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,200
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

147
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:25,600
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

148
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:27,880
Will your money last as long as you do?
